<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kartikeya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Skanda, Murugan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kartikeya, also called Skanda, Murugan and Subrahmanya, is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> god of war, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and Parvati and commander of the army of the gods. Widely worshipped in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, he was the patron deity of the Yaudheya republic and appears on Kushan and Gupta coins. In southern India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, as Murugan, he remains one of the most popular deities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kartikeya.jpg" alt="Kartikeya" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shesmax · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batu_Caves,_Lord_Murugan_Statue._2019-12-01_10-49-22.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fortresses such as Kutaisi, Shorapani, Bagdati and <b>Skanda</b> guarded the passes and river routes, and control of them was often the decisive issue in wars with the Ottomans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Hindu legend tells that Tamil, as Tamil Thāi, “Mother Tamil”, was created by Shiva and brought to the people by <b>Murugan</b> and the sage Agastya.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pattinappalai, one of the Ten Idylls, describes the worship of Vishnu and <b>Murugan</b> alongside Buddhist and Jain monasteries in the Chola capital.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Joyce Flueckinger and Paul Brians call it a &quot;universal aarti&quot;, and Om Lata Bahadur notes its use for gods as different as Krishna, Ganesha and <b>Murugan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/om-jai-jagdish-hare">Om Jai Jagdish Hare</a></li><li><blockquote>Their coins carry the legend Yaudheya ganasya jaya, victory to the Yaudheya republic, and images of <b>Karttikeya</b>, the war god they treated as their divine sovereign.</blockquote><a href="/state/yaudheya-republic">Yaudheya republic</a></li></ul></section>
